In the discussion, James inquired about how long global temperatures would continue to rise if CO₂ emissions ceased immediately. I responded that temperatures would likely stop rising within roughly a decade or two, remaining flat for centuries. Key reasons include the slow decline of CO₂ concentrations and ongoing ocean heat uptake, which largely offset each other. An abrupt cessation of fossil-fuel use could result in near-term extra warming due to the removal of cooling sulfate aerosols. Additionally, ongoing emissions of other greenhouse gases like methane and nitrous oxide would contribute to continued warming unless they were also eliminated. Lastly, I noted that even after temperature stabilization, sea levels, ice loss, and ecosystem impacts would persist for centuries due to the slow response of oceans and ice sheets.
